# Implement-all — no short-win batching

When the engineer asks to **implement all**, **fix all**, **do everything**,
**wire it all**, or equivalent (including “the should-fixes” / a numbered
list of gaps you just proposed):

## Required behavior

1. **Treat the full list as one job.** Every item you named (or they named)
   must be completed, wired end-to-end, and verified before you stop.
2. **Do not stop at a partial win.** No “phase 1 done, rest later” unless
   they explicitly defer an item. Prefer one larger improvement that takes
   longer over several thin batches.
3. **Wire completely.** Code that exists but is not reachable from the
   live path (chat_driver tools, prompts, MCP server, VBA hot-reload,
   driver restart, tests) is **not** done. Ship the connection, not only
   the module.
4. **Verify before claiming done.** Run tests / live smoke / restart as
   required by project sync rules. Cite evidence (test name, log line,
   tool result).
5. **If blocked**, say which remaining item is blocked and why — then
   finish every unblocked item in the same turn sequence. Do not abandon
   the rest of the list.

## Anti-patterns (forbidden under implement-all)

- Shipping only the easiest item and offering to “do the rest next”
- Leaving prompt/tool mismatches (“docs say X, agent has Y”)
- Implementing a helper that nothing calls
- Marking todos complete when wiring or verification is still pending

## Scope note

Normal single-bug / single-feature requests still stay minimal. This rule
applies when they explicitly ask for the **full set** of suggested fixes
or “implement all.”
